\title{How many donors do you actually have?\\[2pt]
\large A short introduction to \textbf{effn}}
\author{Chaohua Tang}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\SweaveOpts{concordance=TRUE, keep.source=FALSE}
\maketitle

<<setup, echo=FALSE, results=hide>>=
library(effn)
set.seed(20261021)
@

\section{What the package answers}

A single-cell differential expression analysis usually reports a sample size
equal to the number of cells. That number is not the number of independent
replicates. \texttt{effn} takes a count matrix with donor labels and returns
three things: the effective number of donors after the depth penalty, the
minimum detectable effect at a chosen power, and the number of additional
donors required to support a target effect.

The package has three functions: \texttt{effn()} does the estimation,
\texttt{prescribe()} inverts it, and \texttt{plot\_effn()} draws the result.

\section{Example 1: a small simulated dataset}

We simulate 120 genes, 8 donors (4 per group) and 25 cells per donor, with a
true 1.4-fold effect on the case group.

<<ex1sim>>=
sim <- local({
  G <- 120; nd <- 8; cells <- 25
  donors <- paste0("d", seq_len(nd))
  grp <- rep(c("ctrl", "case"), each = nd / 2)
  cell_donor <- rep(donors, each = cells)
  mu <- exp(rnorm(G, 2.2, 0.8))
  b <- matrix(rnorm(G * nd, 0, 0.45), nrow = G)
  eff <- ifelse(grp == "case", log2(1.4), 0)
  base <- exp(outer(log(mu), rep(1, nd)) + b + outer(rep(1, G), eff))
  cnt <- matrix(0, G, length(cell_donor))
  for (j in seq_along(cell_donor)) {
    cnt[, j] <- rnbinom(G, mu = base[, match(cell_donor[j], donors)], size = 10)
  }
  rownames(cnt) <- paste0("g", seq_len(G))
  list(counts = cnt, donor = cell_donor,
       group = rep(grp, each = cells))
})
dim(sim$counts)
@

The call takes the matrix, the group of each cell and the donor of each cell.

<<ex1fit>>=
rep <- effn(sim$counts, sim$group, sim$donor, n_boot = 100, n_tech = 10)
rep
@

The printed line \texttt{n\_eff} is the number of donors that survive the depth
penalty. It is bounded above by the number of donors, never by the number of
cells. \texttt{loss} splits the total loss into the part caused by treating
cells as replicates and the part caused by limited depth.

\section{Example 2: the prescription}

\texttt{prescribe()} answers the question the package exists for: how many more
donors are needed to detect a given effect?

<<ex2>>=
p15 <- prescribe(rep, target_mde = log2(1.5))
p15$n_required
p15$n_current
p15$shortfall
p15$sentence
@

The interval comes from resampling donors, not cells, so it reflects the
uncertainty that actually limits the design.

\section{Example 3: a single gene and the plot}

<<ex3>>=
head(prescribe(rep, target_mde = log2(1.5), gene = "g1")$sentence)
pdf(NULL)
panels <- plot_effn(rep, target_mde = log2(1.5))
dev.off()
names(panels)
@

\section{Example 4: the 800-cell subsample that ships with the package}

The paper reports a checker that does not need the 145 MB download: 50 cells per
donor and condition from GSE96583, 8 donors, both groups present.

<<ex4load>>=
f <- system.file("extdata", "gse96583_small.rds", package = "effn")
small <- readRDS(f)
dim(small$counts)
table(small$donor, small$group)
@

The point estimates depend on \texttt{n\_tech}, the number of within-donor
resamples; \texttt{n\_boot} only moves the confidence intervals. With the seed
used in the paper the medians below are the published ones.

<<ex4fit>>=
small_rep <- effn(small$counts, small$group, small$donor,
                  n_boot = 50, n_tech = 40, seed = 20261021)
unlist(small_rep$summary[c("median_n_eff", "median_mde_log2",
                           "median_rho_tech")])
@

\section{Working with real objects}

\texttt{effn()} accepts a \texttt{SingleCellExperiment} or a Seurat object (v4
or v5); the counts layer is read with the appropriate accessor. The group may be
passed as a vector or as the name of a metadata column, and donor labels are
looked up in the sample metadata first. If they are absent the call stops with
\emph{donor labels unavailable} rather than treating the whole matrix as one
replicate.

<<ex5, eval=FALSE>>=
library(SingleCellExperiment)
sce <- SingleCellExperiment(assays = list(counts = sim$counts),
                            colData = DataFrame(donor = sim$donor,
                                                group = sim$group))
rep2 <- effn(sce, "group")   # column name, same as effn(sce, sim$group)
@

\section{What the numbers mean}

For gene $g$ the donor-level value is $y_{dg} = \log_2(10^6 c_{dg}/L_d + 1)$,
where $c_{dg}$ is the sum of raw counts over the cells of donor $d$. The
observed pooled variance across donors is split into a biological and a
technical part, $\hat\sigma^2_{\mathrm{obs}} = \hat\sigma^2_{\mathrm{bio}} +
\hat\sigma^2_{\mathrm{tech}}$. The technical part is measured, not extrapolated:
the default (\texttt{tech = "bootstrap"}) resamples each donor's own cells with
replacement at the depth that donor has and takes the variance of the resulting
pseudobulk values, so no scaling law enters. \texttt{tech = "splithalf"} is kept
as a cheap alternative; it estimates the variance at half depth and needs a
$1/n$ law to carry it to full depth, which $\log_2(\mathrm{CPM}+1)$ does not
obey. Then
\[
n_{\mathrm{eff}} = \frac{n}{1+\rho}, \qquad
\rho = \frac{\hat\sigma^2_{\mathrm{tech}}}{\hat\sigma^2_{\mathrm{bio}}},
\]
and the minimum detectable effect is
\[
\mathrm{MDE} = \left(t_{1-\alpha/2,\,df} + t_{1-\beta,\,df}\right)
\hat\sigma_{\mathrm{bio}}
\sqrt{\frac{1}{n_{\mathrm{eff},1}} + \frac{1}{n_{\mathrm{eff},2}}},
\qquad df = n_1 + n_2 - 2 ,
\]
for an unpaired design, and $df = n-1$ for a paired one. The quantile belongs
to the design, so $df$ counts donors, not effective donors: the effective
counts already carry the depth penalty into the standard error, and charging it
to the degrees of freedom as well would shrink $df$ by $1+\rho$ and inflate
every MDE.
Exact $t$ quantiles are used; the normal approximation is not.

\end{document}
